    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (tid, '1. A 64-year-old male presents with 30 minutes of substernal crushing chest pressure radiating to the left arm. ECG shows 3 mm ST elevation in leads II, III, aVF with reciprocal depression in I and aVL. Which coronary artery is most likely occluded?', '["Left anterior descending", "Right coronary artery", "Left circumflex", "Left main"]', 1, 'Inferior STEMI (leads II, III, aVF) is caused by occlusion of the right coronary artery (RCA) in ~85% of cases. The RCA supplies the inferior wall and often the RV and AV node.'),
    (tid, '2. A 58-year-old diabetic woman presents with nausea, diaphoresis, and epigastric discomfort for 2 hours. Troponin I is 1.2 ng/mL. ECG shows T-wave inversions in V4-V6. What is the diagnosis?', '["Stable angina", "NSTEMI", "STEMI", "Acute gastritis"]', 1, 'Elevated troponin with ischemic symptoms but WITHOUT ST elevation defines NSTEMI. Diabetic women frequently present with atypical symptoms such as epigastric pain, nausea, or isolated dyspnea.'),
    (tid, '3. Which biomarker rises earliest after acute myocardial injury but lacks cardiac specificity?', '["Troponin I", "CK-MB", "Myoglobin", "BNP"]', 2, 'Myoglobin rises within 1-3 hours but is found in all muscle tissue, making it non-specific. High-sensitivity troponin is now preferred for early detection with superior cardiac specificity.'),
    (tid, '4. A patient with an inferior STEMI receives sublingual nitroglycerin and BP drops from 130/80 to 70/40. What is the most likely explanation?', '["Anaphylaxis to nitroglycerin", "Right ventricular infarction", "Aortic dissection", "Vasovagal syncope"]', 1, 'Inferior STEMIs frequently involve the RV. RV infarcts are preload-dependent; nitrates reduce preload and cause profound hypotension. Always check right-sided leads (V4R) in inferior MI.'),
    (tid, '5. What is the recommended door-to-balloon time goal for primary PCI in STEMI?', '["30 minutes", "60 minutes", "90 minutes", "120 minutes"]', 2, 'ACC/AHA guidelines recommend a door-to-balloon time of ≤90 minutes for primary PCI. If transfer to a PCI-capable facility exceeds 120 minutes, fibrinolytic therapy should be administered.'),
    (tid, '6. A 70-year-old presents with chest pain and a new left bundle branch block (LBBB) on ECG. In the context of acute ischemic symptoms, how should this be managed?', '["Serial troponins and observation", "As a STEMI equivalent requiring emergent reperfusion", "Stress testing within 24 hours", "Discharge with cardiology follow-up"]', 1, 'A new or presumably new LBBB in the setting of acute ischemic symptoms is a STEMI equivalent per ACC/AHA guidelines and warrants emergent reperfusion therapy (PCI or fibrinolytics).'),
    (tid, '7. Which ECG finding differentiates acute pericarditis from STEMI?', '["ST elevation in contiguous leads", "Diffuse concave-up ST elevation with PR depression", "Pathological Q waves", "Hyperacute T waves"]', 1, 'Pericarditis shows diffuse concave-upward ST elevation across multiple vascular territories with PR depression. STEMI shows convex ST elevation in a specific coronary distribution with reciprocal changes.'),
    (tid, '8. A 52-year-old smoker has recurrent chest pain at rest, typically at 4 AM. ECG during pain shows transient ST elevation in V2-V4 that resolves spontaneously. What is the diagnosis?', '["Unstable angina", "Prinzmetal (variant) angina", "Anterior STEMI", "Takotsubo cardiomyopathy"]', 1, 'Prinzmetal angina results from coronary vasospasm, occurs at rest (often early morning), causes transient ST elevation that resolves, and responds to calcium channel blockers and nitrates.'),
    (tid, '9. In dual antiplatelet therapy for ACS, what is the mechanism of clopidogrel?', '["COX-1 inhibition", "P2Y12 ADP receptor inhibition", "GPIIb/IIIa receptor blockade", "Thrombin inhibition"]', 1, 'Clopidogrel irreversibly inhibits the P2Y12 ADP receptor on platelets, preventing ADP-mediated platelet activation and aggregation. Combined with aspirin (COX-1 inhibitor), it provides DAPT.'),
    (tid, '10. A STEMI patient receives alteplase. Two hours later, ST segments have not resolved and chest pain persists. What is the next step?', '["Repeat fibrinolytic therapy", "Rescue PCI", "Add IV heparin bolus", "Observe for another 2 hours"]', 1, 'Failed fibrinolysis (persistent symptoms and <50% ST resolution at 60-90 min) requires rescue PCI. Repeat fibrinolysis increases bleeding risk without improved outcomes.'),
    (tid, '11. Which of the following is an absolute contraindication to fibrinolytic therapy?', '["Age over 75 years", "Active internal bleeding", "History of ischemic stroke 4 months ago", "Severe uncontrolled hypertension"]', 1, 'Active internal bleeding (excluding menses) is an absolute contraindication. Age >75 is a relative contraindication. Ischemic stroke within 3 months is absolute; at 4 months it becomes relative.'),
    (tid, '12. A 45-year-old presents with severe tearing chest pain radiating to the back. BP is 200/110 in the right arm and 160/90 in the left arm. Chest X-ray shows a widened mediastinum. What must be ruled out before giving heparin?', '["Pulmonary embolism", "Aortic dissection", "Pericardial tamponade", "Esophageal rupture"]', 1, 'Aortic dissection presents with tearing pain, BP differential between arms, and widened mediastinum. Anticoagulation is absolutely contraindicated as it worsens bleeding into the false lumen.'),
    (tid, '13. What is the most common cause of death within the first hour of an acute MI?', '["Cardiogenic shock", "Ventricular fibrillation", "Cardiac rupture", "Pulmonary embolism"]', 1, 'Ventricular fibrillation is the most common cause of sudden cardiac death in the first hour of acute MI, occurring before patients reach the hospital. This is why early defibrillation is critical.'),
    (tid, '14. A post-MI patient develops a new harsh holosystolic murmur at the left sternal border with a palpable thrill on day 3. What is the most likely complication?', '["Mitral regurgitation from papillary muscle rupture", "Ventricular septal rupture", "Free wall rupture", "Dressler syndrome"]', 1, 'A new holosystolic murmur with a thrill at the left sternal border 3-5 days post-MI suggests ventricular septal rupture. Papillary muscle rupture causes MR heard best at the apex.'),
    (tid, '15. In Killip classification of acute MI, which class includes cardiogenic shock?', '["Killip I", "Killip II", "Killip III", "Killip IV"]', 3, 'Killip IV = cardiogenic shock (hypotension, oliguria, altered mentation). Killip I = no heart failure. Killip II = rales, S3, JVD. Killip III = frank pulmonary edema.'),
    (tid, '16. Which lead on ECG is most sensitive for detecting right ventricular infarction?', '["Lead III", "Lead V1", "Lead V4R", "Lead aVR"]', 2, 'V4R (right-sided V4) has >90% sensitivity for RV infarction. It should be obtained in ALL patients with inferior STEMI to guide fluid management and avoid nitrates/diuretics.'),
    (tid, '17. A patient with known stable angina now has chest pain at rest lasting 20 minutes. ECG shows no ST changes. Troponin is negative at 0 and 3 hours. What is the diagnosis?', '["STEMI", "NSTEMI", "Unstable angina", "Non-cardiac chest pain"]', 2, 'Unstable angina is defined by new-onset or worsening angina, rest angina, or crescendo pattern WITH negative biomarkers. It represents plaque rupture with non-occlusive thrombus.'),
    (tid, '18. What is the Sgarbossa criteria used for?', '["Diagnosing pericarditis", "Identifying STEMI in the presence of LBBB", "Grading heart failure severity", "Risk stratifying pulmonary embolism"]', 1, 'Sgarbossa criteria identify STEMI in patients with LBBB or ventricular paced rhythms where standard ST analysis is unreliable. Concordant ST elevation ≥1mm is the most specific finding.'),
    (tid, '19. Which medication should be given to ALL patients with suspected ACS in the absence of contraindications?', '["Metoprolol", "Aspirin 162-325 mg chewed", "Nitroglycerin", "Morphine"]', 1, 'Aspirin is the single most important medication in ACS and is the only intervention proven to reduce mortality. It should be chewed for rapid buccal absorption. Dose: 162-325 mg.'),
    (tid, '20. A 68-year-old with anterior STEMI develops sudden severe dyspnea, pink frothy sputum, and bilateral crackles. BP is 80/50. What is the diagnosis?', '["Pneumothorax", "Pulmonary embolism", "Cardiogenic shock with pulmonary edema", "Aspiration pneumonia"]', 2, 'Anterior STEMI (LAD territory) involves the largest myocardial territory. Acute pump failure leads to cardiogenic shock with pulmonary edema (Killip IV). Requires vasopressors, inotropes, and emergent PCI.'),
    (tid, '21. What is the TIMI risk score used for in emergency medicine?', '["Determining need for fibrinolytics in STEMI", "Risk stratifying patients with UA/NSTEMI", "Predicting mortality in cardiogenic shock", "Guiding anticoagulation dosing"]', 1, 'The TIMI risk score stratifies UA/NSTEMI patients into low, intermediate, and high risk for adverse cardiac events at 14 days. Higher scores favor early invasive strategy.'),
    (tid, '22. A patient with an acute MI has persistent hypotension despite IV fluids. PA catheter shows elevated PCWP, low cardiac index, and high SVR. Which medication is most appropriate?', '["Norepinephrine", "Dobutamine", "Phenylephrine", "Normal saline bolus"]', 1, 'High PCWP + low CI + high SVR = cardiogenic shock with pump failure. Dobutamine is an inotrope that increases contractility and cardiac output while reducing SVR through beta-2 effects.'),
    (tid, '23. In the setting of STEMI, what does Wellens syndrome on ECG indicate?', '["RCA occlusion", "Critical LAD stenosis", "Circumflex occlusion", "Right ventricular strain"]', 1, 'Wellens syndrome shows deeply inverted or biphasic T waves in V2-V3 during a pain-free interval, indicating critical proximal LAD stenosis. These patients are at high risk for massive anterior MI.'),
    (tid, '24. A 55-year-old male with STEMI undergoes PCI with stent placement. Which antiplatelet regimen is recommended post-procedure?', '["Aspirin alone lifelong", "Aspirin + clopidogrel for 12 months then aspirin alone", "Clopidogrel alone for 6 months", "Warfarin + aspirin for 3 months"]', 1, 'After PCI with drug-eluting stent for ACS, DAPT (aspirin + P2Y12 inhibitor) is recommended for at least 12 months to prevent stent thrombosis, followed by lifelong aspirin monotherapy.'),
    (tid, '25. Which arrhythmia is the most common cause of hemodynamic compromise following inferior MI?', '["Ventricular tachycardia", "Atrial fibrillation", "Complete heart block", "Ventricular fibrillation"]', 2, 'The RCA supplies the AV node in 85% of patients. Inferior MI can cause AV nodal ischemia leading to complete (3rd degree) heart block. This often responds to atropine and may require temporary pacing.');
